.TH PTY_WAIT_FOR 3 2026-10-03 "libptytest 0.1" "libptytest Manual"
.SH NAME
pty_wait_for, pty_wait_until, pty_settle, pty_drain \- wait for the
program under test
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <ptytest.h>
.PP
.BI "int pty_wait_for(PtySession *" s ", const char *" text ", int " timeout_ms );
.PP
.BI "typedef int (*PtyPredicate)(PtySession *" s ", void *" ctx );
.BI "int pty_wait_until(PtySession *" s ", PtyPredicate " pred ", void *" ctx ,
.BI "                   int " timeout_ms );
.PP
.BI "int pty_settle(PtySession *" s ", int " quiet_ms );
.BI "int pty_drain(PtySession *" s );
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The screen changes only while the library reads the program's output.
These functions read it, feed it to the emulator, and return when a
condition holds or a deadline passes.
None of them sleeps for a fixed time.
.PP
.BR pty_wait_for ()
returns as soon as some row of the screen contains
.IR text .
It checks the screen first, so text that is already shown satisfies it at
once \(em including text left over from the previous view.
Wait for something the
.I new
view shows, not something both views share.
.PP
.BR pty_wait_until ()
returns as soon as
.IR pred ( s ,
.IR ctx )
returns non-zero; it is checked before waiting and after every read.
.PP
.BR pty_settle ()
returns once no output has arrived for
.I quiet_ms
milliseconds.
Use it after a
.BR pty_wait_for ()
when the program draws a view in several writes and the rest of the view
has to be complete before a full-screen comparison.
.PP
.BR pty_drain ()
processes whatever output is pending, without waiting for more.
.SH RETURN VALUE
.BR pty_wait_for ()
and
.BR pty_wait_until ()
return 0 when the condition holds, \-1 on timeout, when the child closed
the PTY without satisfying it, or for invalid arguments.
.PP
.BR pty_settle ()
returns 0 when the output paused, \-1 if the child closed the PTY, if
there is no child, or if the output did not pause within 30 seconds.
.PP
.BR pty_drain ()
returns the number of bytes processed.
.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
static int cursor_on_row_5(PtySession *s, void *ctx)
{
    int row, col;
    (void)ctx;
    pty_cursor(s, &row, &col);
    return row == 5;
}

pty_send_key(s, PTY_KEY_DOWN);
ASSERT(pty_wait_until(s, cursor_on_row_5, NULL, 3000) == 0,
       "the cursor moved to row 5");
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pty_wait_region (3),
.BR pty_wait_exit (3),
.BR pty_feed (3),
.BR pty_assert (3)
